ld see a shrink but  most candidates will need to raise some  serious cash in 2016 candidates and  outside groups spent nearly two and a  half billion dollars on the race to the  White House  that's according to election watchdog  open secrets those planning to run also  need to attract talented staffers and  that can be tough when there's a large  pool of potential candidates senator  Bernie Sanders recently held a Vermont  summit through his Sanders Institute  that brought together staffers and  supporters from 2016 and politicians  like Cory Booker Jeff Merkley and  Kirsten Gillibrand have reportedly  already reached out to potential hires  in key primary States politicians are  also making the rounds with both media  tours and road trips to key states like  Iowa where the state's early caucus can  make or break a candidate that includes  former Vice President Joe Biden senator  Cory Booker Congressman John Delaney and  governor Steve Bullock and splashy book  tours like those launched by Senators  Kamala Harris and Elizabeth Warren also  raised a candidate's profile the tours  helped politicians network with key  supporters and build name recognition to  essential ingredients for a presidential  campaign and finally there's the  political platform candidates need to be  clear about where they stand on the  issues before they launch a campaign  sitting representatives and senators  might introduce legislation to establish  their positions or sit on key committees  this is about the closest I'll probably  ever have in my life to an I am  Spartacus moment and many looking to  enter the race have already launched  websites and published videos that  explain what they're fighting for and  while both parties seem to focus on  fighting I'm focusing on the future in  total at least two dozen Democrats are  thinking about running and after they  battle each other for the nomination one  will ultimately face President Trump  [Music]
 

